  {
    label: 'Do this',
    title: 'Get better at explanation, not only learning',
    body:
      'Average-college students often close the gap faster by learning how to explain projects, decisions, bugs, and outcomes clearly.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    number: '01',
    title: 'Waiting too long to choose a direction',
    body:
      'BCA is broad enough that you can lose two years just saying yes to everything. Pick a direction early enough to build useful proof.',
  },
  {
    number: '02',
    title: 'Collecting certificates instead of building evidence',
    body:
      'Certificates can support a profile, but they rarely replace projects, internships, bug-fixes, dashboards, labs, or real problem-solving.',
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    title: 'Chasing developer roles without liking developer work',
    body:
      'Software development is a strong path, but only when the work fits you. Many students would do better in QA, support, cloud, analyst, or product-support roles.',
  },
  {
    number: '04',
    title: 'Using MCA as a delay tactic',
    body:
      'MCA can be useful. It becomes expensive confusion when you choose it only because you still feel unsure after BCA and have not fixed the underlying direction problem.',
  },
  {
    number: '05',
    title: 'Ignoring communication and documentation',
    body:
      'Tech roles still reward clear written thinking. Students who can explain their work often outperform students with similar technical level but weaker communication.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Is BCA a good degree for career growth?',
    answer:
      'BCA can be a good degree when you use it as a platform for a real skill stack. The degree by itself is usually not enough. The outcome depends on whether you move into a specific path like development, testing, support, data, systems, or a later MCA-led specialisation.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What are the best BCA career options after graduation?',
    answer:
      'The best BCA career options depend on fit. Common strong routes include software development, web development, QA or testing, technical support, cloud or IT operations support, data analyst support, and product or digital operations roles.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I get a software job after BCA without MCA?',
    answer:
      'Yes, but you usually need stronger proof. A BCA student with real projects, GitHub work, deployment experience, debugging ability, and interview-ready basics often has a better shot than a student who only has the degree label.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is MCA necessary after BCA?',
    answer:
      'No. MCA is useful for some students, especially if they want deeper technical grounding, a reset, or stronger academic signalling. But many BCA students can still build careers without MCA if they create skill depth and real proof early.',
  },
  {
    question: 'BCA or BTech which is better?',
    answer:
      'BTech usually gives stronger engineering signalling and access to some hiring pipelines. BCA can still work very well when your goal is a practical computing path and you are ready to build your own portfolio aggressively. The better path depends on context, cost, fit, and the quality of execution.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which BCA career options fit students who do not want coding all day?',
    answer:
      'QA, application support, cloud support, technical operations, data reporting, implementation, and product-support roles are often better fits for students who like tech environments but do not want full-time feature development.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I learn in the first year of BCA?',
    answer:
      'Start with one programming language, Git and GitHub, problem-solving basics, web fundamentals, and communication. If you already know you are more analyst-leaning, add Excel and SQL early too.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can BCA students use internships and apprenticeships to get ahead?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Internships and apprenticeships are one of the best ways to turn BCA into real employability. Official channels like the AICTE Internship Portal, Apprenticeship India, NATS, and National Career Service are worth checking instead of depending only on campus placement.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What if I am weak in maths but interested in BCA career options?',
    answer:
      'Weak maths does not automatically block every BCA path. Many students still do better in web development, QA, support, implementation, reporting, and other logic-heavy practical roles. The more important question is whether you can build consistency, reasoning, and proof.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I start in support or QA and move into higher-level technical roles later?',
    answer:
      'Yes, but the move usually happens only when you deliberately deepen your stack. Support can move toward cloud, infra, or product operations. QA can move toward automation. Reporting roles can move toward stronger data work. The first role does not lock your whole future, but passive years will.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I do in each BCA year to improve career options?',
    answer:
