const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: item.answer,
    },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>BCA career options include software development, web development, QA, tech support, data roles, and MCA-led specialisation paths.</strong>
    The degree gives you a computing foundation, not a finished specialisation — the real outcome depends on which track you go deep in and the proof you build. Treated deliberately, BCA can become the base of a genuine high-income skill portfolio rather than just a starter job title.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>BCA opens real paths into software development, web development, QA, tech support, and data roles — not only generic "IT" jobs.</li>
      <li>The degree gives you a computing foundation (programming, databases, networks, OS basics), not a finished specialisation employers hire for directly.</li>
      <li>Choose between BCA, BTech, and BSc Computer Science by the track you want to go deep in, not by which degree name sounds stronger.</li>
      <li>BCA without MCA can still work well — MCA only makes sense in specific situations, not by default.</li>
      <li><strong>Run your BCA path through the 4-Checkpoint Protocol and 3 Gates before committing hard to one specialisation.</strong></li>
      <li>Do not aim only for the first developer or support job. Combine deep technical skill with AI-assisted workflow design, communication, product or industry context, and deployed proof that can later support specialist, freelance, consulting, or product income.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <section class="jump-nav" aria-labelledby="jump-nav-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">BCA guide</p>
    <div class="jump-nav__top">
      <h2 id="jump-nav-title">What this article covers</h2>
      <p>
        BCA career options include software development, web development, QA, tech support, data roles, and MCA-led specialisation paths.
        This guide helps you choose the right one instead of blindly chasing the most hyped one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="jump-nav__grid">
      {jumpLinks.map((link) => (
        <a href={`#${link.id}`}>{link.label}</a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reality first</p>
    <h2>Why most BCA advice goes wrong</h2>
    <p>
      Most BCA advice fails because it talks about the degree, not the actual work.
    </p>
    <p>
      It says things like “BCA has many scope” or “after BCA you can go into IT”.
    </p>
    <p>
      That sounds positive, but it is too vague to help you decide anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      Real decisions are more specific.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do you want to build software, debug systems, analyse data, support products, or deepen your route through MCA?
    </p>
    <p>
      Those paths need different skills, different proof, and different patience.
    </p>

    <div class="reality-grid">
      {realityCards.map((card) => (
        <article class="reality-card">
          <p class="mini-label">{card.label}</p>
          <h3>{card.title}</h3>
          <p>{card.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="what-bca">
    <p class="eyebrow">Degree signal</p>
    <h2>What BCA actually gives you</h2>
    <p>
      BCA usually gives you a computing foundation, not a finished specialisation.
    </p>
    <p>
      That foundation often includes programming basics, databases, computer networks, operating systems, software concepts, and application-focused coursework.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is useful.
    </p>
    <p>
      But the degree name alone does not automatically tell employers what you can do well.
    </p>
    <p>
      Employers still want evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Can you build?
    </p>
    <p>
      Can you debug?
    </p>
    <p>
      Can you analyse?
    </p>
    <p>
      Can you support real systems without freezing?
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout--gold">
      <p class="callout__label">Honest take</p>
      <p>
        BCA becomes a strong path when you stop treating it like a passive degree and start treating it like a launchpad.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="paths">
    <p class="eyebrow">Role paths</p>
    <h2>Real BCA career options</h2>
    <p>
      The strongest BCA career options are the ones where your skill stack, work style, and proof start to line up.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here is what the most practical route clusters usually look like.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="paths-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Path</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
            <th>First step</th>
            <th>Reality check</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {pathRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Path"><strong>{row.path}</strong></td>
              <td data-label="Best for">{row.bestFor}</td>
              <td data-label="First step">{row.firstStep}</td>
              <td data-label="Reality check">{row.reality}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note-line">
      The best route is not automatically the one with the most hype.
      It is the one where you can build enough competence and enough staying power to keep growing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="specialise">
    <h2>The highest-demand tracks to go deep in</h2>
    <p>
      Listing roles is easy. The students who stand out usually pick one track and go genuinely deep, instead of staying shallow across many.
    </p>
    <p>
      These tracks are in strong demand right now, and each has a clear first skill to build.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="paths-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Track</th>
            <th>What to learn first</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            { t: 'Full-stack web development', s: 'One language well, then a front-end framework and a back-end with a real database' },
            { t: 'Data analytics', s: 'SQL and spreadsheets first, then Python with Pandas and basic visualisation' },
            { t: 'Cloud and DevOps', s: 'Linux basics, one cloud platform, then Git, Docker, and CI/CD fundamentals' },
            { t: 'Cybersecurity', s: 'Networking and OS basics, then hands-on labs and security fundamentals' },
            { t: 'AI-assisted development and QA', s: 'Solid coding plus using AI tools well, with testing and clear documentation habits' },
          ].map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Track"><strong>{row.t}</strong></td>
              <td data-label="What to learn first">{row.s}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note-line">
      Pick one, build two real projects in it, and you will usually compete far better than a student who dabbled in everything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="compare">
    <p class="eyebrow">Degree choice</p>
    <h2>BCA vs BTech vs BSc Computer Science</h2>
    <p>
      Students often compare these as if they are only subject-name differences.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are not.
    </p>
    <p>
      They differ in signalling, depth, campus access, cost, and how much self-driven skill-building you usually need.
    </p>

    <div class="comparison-grid">
