          <h3>{card.title}</h3>
          <p>{card.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="without-mca">
    <p class="eyebrow">No delay</p>
    <h2>BCA without MCA can still work</h2>
    <p>
      You do not have to assume that BCA is incomplete unless you add MCA.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many students can still build a real path after BCA without going straight into another degree.
    </p>
    <p>
      This becomes more realistic when you do three things early.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Pick a narrower target role instead of a vague “IT career”.</li>
      <li>Build visible proof before graduation panic starts.</li>
      <li>Improve your explanation skills, not just your technical notes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you can already show working projects, real debugging, useful documentation, dashboards, or strong support thinking, you are not empty just because you do not have MCA yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mca">
    <p class="eyebrow">Further study</p>
    <h2>When MCA makes sense after BCA</h2>
    <p>
      MCA can be a good move.
    </p>
    <p>
      But it should solve a real problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      It makes more sense when you want deeper technical grounding, stronger academic signalling, or a sharper reset into development-heavy or more advanced computing roles.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are targeting MCA, keep an eye on the <a href="https://nimcet.admissions.nic.in/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">official NIMCET portal</a> for entrance-process details.
    </p>
    <p>
      But do not use MCA as an excuse to postpone choosing a direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      MCA helps most when you already know why you are doing it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before committing to an expensive private MCA seat, test it against a simple budget rule: as a strict
      planning heuristic, most further-education spend on top of your first degree should stay within a
      conservative slice of your total education budget, and you should be able to name what the remaining
      budget could otherwise fund — projects, certifications, a laptop upgrade, or a longer job-search runway.
      Spending materially more only makes sense with a specific, checkable reason: strong placement outcomes you
      can verify, respected faculty or research access, or an accreditation the specific role actually requires
      — not general brand reputation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="skills">
    <p class="eyebrow">Skill stack</p>
    <h2>Skills that raise BCA outcomes the most</h2>
    <p>
      BCA results improve when your learning moves from subject names to usable skill clusters.
    </p>

    <div class="skills-grid">
      {skillCards.map((card) => (
        <article class="skill-card">
          <p class="mini-label">{card.label}</p>
          <h3>{card.title}</h3>
          <p>{card.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
      <p>
        A simple rule helps here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pick one primary path and one support layer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Example: developer plus Git and deployment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Or analyst plus SQL and dashboards.
      </p>
      <p>
        Or support plus Linux and networking.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is how a plain BCA transcript turns into a real high-income skill portfolio — one deep primary skill, one support layer, and visible proof of both, compounding toward financial freedom faster than the degree alone ever could.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>This is what actually raises your income ceiling over time: the right skill mix for you, visible proof of work, the ability to explain it clearly, and an honest read of how you are positioned against other candidates</strong> — not the degree name alone. Most of the tools above are free or near-free to start with (official documentation, freeCodeCamp-style courses, YouTube walkthroughs, and practice projects); only pay for a structured course once you have tested the direction yourself and know a paid version adds real mentorship, feedback, or accountability you cannot get for free.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout--gold">
      <p class="callout__label">Personal fit and financial reality</p>
      <p>
        Skill choice is not only a technical decision. Weigh it against your current financial runway, family
        pressure, and how long you can realistically sustain a "still learning" phase before you need income.
        A slightly slower path that fits your actual life is usually stronger than a faster path you cannot
        sustain.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="late-start">
    <p class="eyebrow">Late start filter</p>
    <h2>If maths is weak or you started coding late, do not panic too early</h2>
    <p>
      Many BCA students reject themselves too early.
    </p>
    <p>
      They assume weak maths, average school marks, or a late coding start means the whole field is closed.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is usually too extreme.
    </p>
    <p>
      The better question is which BCA path still gives you a realistic way to build competence from where you are now.
    </p>

    <div class="step-stack">
      {lateStartSteps.map((step) => (
        <article class="step-card">
          <p class="mini-label">{step.label}</p>
          <h3>{step.title}</h3>
          <p>{step.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout--gold">
      <p class="callout__label">Important distinction</p>
      <p>
        Struggling with advanced maths is not the same as having no future in computing.
        But avoiding logic practice altogether is still a problem.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="checkpoint">
